    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0161",
      "sequence": 173,
      "meaning": "The king replies that treasures of wisdom like this sage conceal themselves with humble disguises and dissolved pride; therefore saints and scripture proclaim the utterly possessionless dear to Hari. Even Brahmā and Śiva might doubt that one so destitute, homeless and beggarly could exist. He bows to the sage’s feet and begs grace. Seeing the king’s natural affection and special trust, the pretender takes him fully into confidence and says that he has lived there for ages without meeting anyone or revealing himself, because worldly fame burns up austerity like fire consumes a forest. Tulsīdāsa warns that fools and even clever people are misled by a handsome guise: see the beautiful peacock, whose nectar-like words conceal a diet of snakes.",
