    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0164",
      "sequence": 176,
      "meaning": "The pretender names Pratāpabhānu, his father Satyaketu, and says a teacher’s grace makes everything known, though one should not reveal such knowledge uselessly. The king’s natural simplicity, trust and political wisdom have awakened affection, so he will answer whatever is asked and grant a desired boon. Delighted, Pratāpabhānu clasps his feet and says the sage’s sight has placed the four human aims in his palm. Yet, seeing him pleased, he requests an almost impossible boon: a body free from age and death, invincibility in battle, and single-sovereign, enemyless rule over the earth for a hundred cosmic ages.",
